## static readonly — shared across instances

```ts
class HttpClient {
  static readonly MAX_RETRIES = 3;
  static readonly BASE_URL = 'https://api.example.com';
}

HttpClient.MAX_RETRIES; // 3
```

`static` puts the member on the class itself, not each instance. `readonly` blocks reassignment after initialisation. Together they express a true constant.

## Per-instance constants

When the value is fixed for an instance but differs between instances, drop `static`:

```ts
class User {
  readonly id: string;
  constructor(id: string) { this.id = id; } // set once, then frozen
}
```

## Keep the literal type with as const

A `readonly` field still widens its type — `static readonly MODE = 'dark'` is typed `string`. Add `as const` to pin the literal:

```ts
class Theme {
  static readonly MODE = 'dark' as const; // type 'dark', not string
}
```

## Or just use a module constant

If the value does not conceptually belong to the class, a plain module-level `const` is simpler and tree-shakes better:

```ts
export const MAX_RETRIES = 3;
```

Class constants earn their place when they are part of the class's public API or are referenced via the class name for clarity.

